.TH me "" "Command"
.SH NAME
me \- MicroEMACS screen editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBme [\fIfile\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
me -- MicroEMACS screen editor
Usage:	me [ file ]
With no 'file', starts editing an empty buffer named 'main'.
.HE
.B me
is MicroEMACS, a screen editor that keeps the text being edited in one
or more memory buffers, each shown in its own window on the screen.
If
.I file
is given,
.B me
reads it into a buffer named after
.IR file 's
last component and displays it in a window; if
.I file
does not exist,
.B me
starts an empty buffer under that name so the file can be created by
saving it later.
With no argument,
.B me
starts with a single empty buffer named
.BR main .
.PP
Ordinary printable characters typed are inserted at the current
position, which lies between two characters rather than on one.
Control characters and the two-character sequences beginning with
.B ESC
(the meta prefix) or
.B ^X
(the control-X prefix) invoke editing commands, listed below by
category.
.B ^Q
inserts the following character literally, even if it is one of
these command characters.
.SH "Moving the Cursor"
.IP \fB^A\fR
Move to the start of the line.
.IP \fB^B\fR
Move backward one character.
.IP "\fBESC B\fR"
Move backward one word.
.IP \fB^E\fR
Move to the end of the line.
.IP \fB^F\fR
Move forward one character.
.IP "\fBESC F\fR"
Move forward one word.
.IP \fB^N\fR
Move to the next line.
.IP \fB^P\fR
Move to the previous line.
.IP \fB^V\fR
Move forward one page (screen).
.IP "\fBESC V\fR"
Move backward one page.
.IP "\fBESC <\fR"
Move to the start of the buffer.
.IP "\fBESC >\fR"
Move to the end of the buffer.
.IP "\fBESC !\fR"
Reposition the window so the current line appears at the row given by
the numeric argument (top if positive, bottom if negative, middle if
zero).
.SH "Killing, Deleting, and Yanking"
.IP \fB^D\fR
Delete the next character (forward delete).
.IP \fB^H\fR
Delete the previous character (backward delete); with an argument,
delete that many previous characters.
.IP \fB<DEL>\fR
Same as
.BR ^H .
.IP "\fBESC ^H\fR"
Delete the previous word.
.IP "\fBESC <DEL>\fR"
Same as
.BR "ESC ^H" .
.IP "\fBESC D\fR"
Delete the next word.
.IP \fB^K\fR
Kill (delete, saving into the kill buffer) from the current position
to the end of the line.
.IP \fB^W\fR
Kill the region between the mark and the current position.
.IP "\fBESC W\fR"
Copy the region between the mark and the current position into the
kill buffer without deleting it.
.IP \fB^Y\fR
Yank the kill buffer back into the text at the current position.
.IP \fB^T\fR
Transpose the two characters on either side of the current position.
.IP "\fB^X ^O\fR"
Delete blank lines around the current position.
.SH Marking
.IP \fB^@\fR
Set the mark at the current position.
.IP "\fBESC .\fR"
Same as
.BR ^@ .
.IP "\fBESC X ^X\fR"
Set the mark at the current position, then move to the previous mark
(exchange point and mark).
.SH "Windows"
.IP "\fB^X 1\fR"
Make the current window the only window on the screen.
.IP "\fB^X 2\fR"
Split the current window into two.
.IP "\fB^X N\fR"
Move to the next window.
.IP "\fB^X P\fR"
Move to the previous window.
.IP "\fB^X ^N\fR"
Move the text in the current window down.
.IP "\fB^X ^P\fR"
Move the text in the current window up.
.IP "\fB^X Z\fR"
Enlarge the current window.
.IP "\fB^X ^Z\fR"
Shrink the current window.
.IP \fB^L\fR
Redraw (refresh) the screen.
.SH "Buffers and Files"
.IP "\fB^X B\fR"
Select a buffer by name for display in the current window.
.IP "\fB^X K\fR"
Delete (kill) a named buffer.
.IP "\fB^X ^B\fR"
List all buffers, with their change flag, size, and associated file
name.
.IP "\fB^X ^F\fR"
Change the file name associated with the current buffer.
.IP "\fB^X ^R\fR"
Read a named file into a new buffer, for reading only.
.IP "\fB^X ^V\fR"
Visit a named file: read it into a new buffer for reading and
writing.
.IP "\fB^X ^S\fR"
Save the current buffer to its associated file, if it has changed.
.IP "\fB^X ^W\fR"
Write the current buffer to a named file.
.IP \fB^Z\fR
Save the current buffer if changed, then exit
.BR me .
.IP "\fB^X ^C\fR"
Exit
.B me
without saving; asks for confirmation if any buffer has unsaved
changes, unless given a numeric argument.
.SH "Search"
.IP \fB^R\fR
Incremental search backward; the search progresses as each character
of the pattern is typed.
.IP \fB^S\fR
Incremental search forward.
.IP "\fBESC R\fR"
Search backward for a complete pattern read on the message line.
.IP "\fBESC S\fR"
Search forward for a complete pattern read on the message line.
.SH "Case Conversion"
.IP "\fBESC C\fR"
Capitalize the next word.
.IP "\fBESC L\fR"
Lower-case the next word.
.IP "\fBESC U\fR"
Upper-case the next word.
.IP "\fB^X ^L\fR"
Lower-case the region between the mark and the current position.
.IP "\fB^X ^U\fR"
Upper-case the region between the mark and the current position.
.SH "Keyboard Macros"
.IP "\fB^X (\fR"
Begin defining a keyboard macro; everything typed up to
.B "^X )"
is recorded.
.IP "\fB^X )\fR"
End the keyboard macro definition.
.IP "\fB^X E\fR"
Execute the keyboard macro.
.SH "Running Other Commands"
.IP \fB^C\fR
Suspend
.B me
and run an interactive command interpreter (subshell); exiting the
subshell returns to
.BR me .
.IP "\fB^X !\fR"
Prompt for a single shell command, run it, and show its output.
.SH "Miscellaneous"
.IP \fB^G\fR
Abort the command in progress.
.IP \fB^I\fR
Insert a tab.
.IP \fB^J\fR
Insert a new line and indent it to the current level.
.IP \fB^M\fR
Insert a new line (carriage return).
.IP \fB^O\fR
Open a blank line at the current position.
.IP \fB^Q\fR
Insert the next character literally.
.IP "\fBESC Q\fR"
Insert a character given by its numeric value.
.IP \fB^U\fR
Introduce a numeric argument for the next command; typed alone it
sets the argument to 4, and repeating
.B ^U
multiplies it by 4 each time.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B me
writes error and informational messages on the last line of the
screen.
It keeps all editing in memory and does not alter the file on disk
until the buffer is saved.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B ed
.SH NOTES
.B me
is derived from the public-domain MicroEMACS editor by Dave Conroy.
